# El Niño Forecaster with Winter Impacts Map: full build guide

Written assuming you know how to code but know nothing about weather. Every weather term is explained the first time it shows up, every dataset has an exact download location, and the build is broken into small steps.

---

## Part 1. The weather concepts, in plain English

You need five ideas. That is genuinely all.

**Sea surface temperature (SST).** The temperature of the top layer of the ocean. Scientists have gridded maps of it going back over a century, one value per cell of the ocean, one map per month. Think of each monthly map as a grayscale image where pixel brightness is how warm that patch of ocean was.

**El Niño and La Niña (together called ENSO).** Every few years, a huge stretch of the tropical Pacific Ocean, along the equator, gets unusually warm or unusually cold. The warm phase is called El Niño, the cold phase is La Niña, and the whole seesaw is called ENSO, short for El Niño Southern Oscillation. It matters because that warm or cold patch changes the path of the jet stream, which shifts where storms go for an entire winter across North America and beyond. So a patch of warm ocean water near the equator in the Pacific can mean a wetter California and a milder Canadian winter months later. That delay is the whole reason forecasting is possible.

**The Niño 3.4 region.** To turn "the Pacific is warm" into a number, scientists agreed on one specific rectangle of ocean along the equator, called the Niño 3.4 region. It sits from 5 degrees north to 5 degrees south, and from 170 degrees west to 120 degrees west. They average the SST in just that box.

**Anomaly.** This is the single most important word in the project. An anomaly is not the temperature, it is how far the temperature is from normal for that time of year. The ocean is naturally warmer in some months than others, so raw temperature is useless for spotting El Niño. Instead you compute, for each month, the average over many years (the "climatology"), and subtract it. What is left, the anomaly, is the departure from normal. Plus 2 means two degrees warmer than a typical version of that month. Everything in this project runs on anomalies, not raw values.

**ONI, the Oceanic Niño Index.** This is the official number that decides whether we are in El Niño. Take the SST anomaly in the Niño 3.4 box, average it over three months in a row to smooth out noise, and that running value is the ONI. The rule NOAA uses: ONI of plus 0.5 or higher means El Niño, minus 0.5 or lower means La Niña, in between is neutral. When the news says "a strong El Niño," they mean the ONI is high. Right now it is around plus 2.3, which is firmly El Niño.

So the chain is: ocean temperature maps, turned into anomalies, averaged over one special box, smoothed over three months, gives the ONI, which tells you the ENSO state.

---

## Part 2. The machine learning concepts, briefly

**Forecasting the ONI as a prediction problem.** You are going to teach a model this task: given the last twelve monthly SST anomaly maps of the tropical Pacific, output the ONI a few months from now. Input is a stack of maps, output is one number. That is a standard supervised learning setup, just with images as input.

**CNN, convolutional neural network.** A type of neural network built for grid shaped data like images. Instead of looking at every pixel independently, it slides small filters across the image to pick up local patterns (an edge, a warm blob), then combines them into bigger patterns. It is the natural choice here because SST maps are images, and the shape and position of the warm blob is exactly the signal. You will stack the twelve input months as twelve channels, the same way a color image has three channels, and let the network read the whole sequence at once.

**Baseline.** A dumb method you must beat to prove your model did anything. The main one here is persistence, which just guesses that the ONI a few months from now equals the ONI today. It is weak at long range but hard to beat at short range, so it keeps you honest.

**Train, validation, test split, and leakage.** You split your history into three time periods. You fit the model on the earliest (train), tune it on the middle (validation), and judge it on the most recent, untouched period (test). Crucially you split by time, never randomly, because weather months next to each other are almost identical, so a random split would let the model peek at answers. That peeking is called leakage, and it makes results look great and mean nothing.

**Skill.** How much better than the baseline you are. The headline chart of the project is skill versus lead time: for forecasts 1, 3, 6, 9 months out, how well does the model track reality compared to persistence. A model that clearly beats persistence out to some horizon and then fades is an honest, respectable result.

---

## Part 3. What the project is, now that you have the words

Two layers.

Layer one, the machine learning, is a CNN that reads twelve months of tropical Pacific SST anomaly maps and forecasts the ONI several months ahead. You train it on decades of history, test it on recent held out years, and score it against persistence to produce a skill curve. This is a small version of a real 2019 Nature paper (Ham, Kim, Luo) that forecast ENSO with a CNN.

Layer two, the demo, takes the model's forecast for the coming winter and turns it into something a normal person feels. Using the historical link between the ONI and each city's past winters, you predict whether a given city is heading for a warmer, colder, wetter, drier, snowier, or less snowy winter, and you show it on a map. Click your city, read a plain sentence, see a confidence tag that is honest about where the signal is real.

The forecaster is the substance. The map is the hook.

---

## Part 4. Where every piece of data comes from

Three sources. All free, all public, no accounts needed for the first two and none at all for the third.

### 4a. Ocean temperature maps, for the forecaster

**Dataset:** NOAA ERSSTv5. Monthly sea surface temperature on a 2 degree by 2 degree global grid, going back to 1854.

**Landing page:** https://psl.noaa.gov/data/gridded/data.noaa.ersst.v5.html

**The actual file** is called `sst.mnmean.nc` (a netCDF file, a common scientific array format). You have two ways to get it:

- Direct download: from the landing page click the netCDF download link, or fetch it directly from the PSL download server. The path follows the pattern `https://downloads.psl.noaa.gov/Datasets/noaa.ersst.v5/sst.mnmean.nc`.
- No download at all: open it straight from NOAA over the network with xarray using their OPeNDAP link `https://psl.noaa.gov/thredds/dodsC/Datasets/noaa.ersst.v5/sst.mnmean.nc`. xarray streams only the slice you ask for.

If a direct link ever moves, the landing page above always has the current one.

**What is inside:** a variable called `sst`, with dimensions time, latitude, longitude. Latitude runs about 88 south to 88 north, longitude 0 to 358, one time step per month from January 1854.

### 4b. The ONI, for the forecaster target and the El Niño labels

**Source:** NOAA Climate Prediction Center.

**The machine readable file:** https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/data/indices/oni.ascii.txt

This is a plain text table. Each row is one three month season. The columns are the season code (`SEAS`, values like DJF, JFM, FMA and so on), the year (`YR`), the total SST value (`TOTAL`), and the anomaly (`ANOM`). That `ANOM` column is the ONI. Use it two ways: as the number your model predicts, and, by checking whether it is at or above plus 0.5, as the label for which winters were El Niño.

Human readable color coded version, handy for sanity checking: https://www.cpc.ncep.noaa.gov/products/analysis_monitoring/ensostuff/ONI_v5.php

One alignment note for later: each ONI season is centered on its middle month, so DJF is centered on January, JFM on February, and so on. When you line the ONI up with monthly SST, map each season to its middle month.

### 4c. Per city winter weather, for the impacts map

**Source:** Open-Meteo historical archive. Free, no API key, based on the ERA5 reanalysis, covers 1940 to present, returns data for any latitude and longitude as JSON.

**Endpoint:** `https://archive-api.open-meteo.com/v1/archive`

**Example request** for one city, pulling daily mean temperature, total precipitation, and total snowfall:

```
https://archive-api.open-meteo.com/v1/archive?latitude=43.65&longitude=-79.38&start_date=1960-01-01&end_date=2025-12-31&daily=temperature_2m_mean,precipitation_sum,snowfall_sum&timezone=auto
```

You call this once per city in your list, with that city's coordinates. No key, no login.

